1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Open synthetic clinic, household, and district datasets through QGIS's vector-data loader.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Three clinics and 144 households, with district boundaries in a coordinate system measured in metres.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Choose the clinics layer in QGIS's built-in Buffer algorithm.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Create a 1,000-metre service area around each clinic.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Use 24 segments per quadrant and dissolve the buffers into one coverage feature.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Run the buffer calculation and write a permanent GeoPackage layer.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
The resulting polygons show the combined clinic service areas.

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Use Count points in polygon to measure household coverage within the saved service area.

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Count the household points and name the output column HOUSEHOLDS.

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Run the count and save the geometry and result together in a second GeoPackage.

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:01,000
The attribute table shows 78. Independent checks of the saved polygons reproduce the same household membership.

12
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Bring household and clinic markers above the filled service areas.

13
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Show the complete district map and the combined clinic coverage.

14
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Relative file paths keep the saved map connected to its accompanying datasets.

15
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Save clinic-coverage.qgz with the source and analysis layers preserved.

16
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Project and GeoPackages saved. Coverage independently checked against all 144 synthetic households.
